        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Nerve impulses can be transmitted along a myelinated motor neurone to a neuromuscular junction at fast speeds of up to 100 m s<sup>–1</sup>.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 26
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Outline how a transmission speed of 100 m s<sup>–1</sup> is achieved by the neurone.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 3,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "15.1(7)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 3,
          "instruction": "<i>any</i><b><i> three </i></b><i>from :</i><b><i> </i></b>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "1  myelin <b>sheath</b> / Schwann cells, acts as an insulator / prevents movement of ions <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "2  depolarisation / action potential, <b>only</b> occurs at the nodes <b>; I</b> impulses",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "3  action potentials jump from node to node / saltatory conduction <b>; I</b> impulses<b> </b> <b> </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "4  long(er) local circuits <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "5  AVP <b>;</b> e.g. wide axon diameter",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Myelin limits ion exchange between nodes. Local currents bring the next node to threshold, so action potentials are regenerated at successive nodes.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:15.1(7)",
                "ms:a#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Locate myelinated regions and nodes.",
            "Explain how local currents reduce the number of membrane regions that must depolarise."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Describing an action potential as jumping through empty space",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:15.1(7)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 26,
          "ms": 22
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "When a nerve impulse reaches the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine is released and diffuses to the sarcolemma.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 26
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Describe how the release of acetylcholine can result in the binding of calcium ions to troponin in the sarcomere.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 4,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "15.1(10)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 4,
          "instruction": "<i>any</i><b><i> four </i></b><i>from :</i><b><i> </i></b>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "1  ACh binds to receptors on <u>sarcolemma</u> <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "2  (ligand-gated) Na<sup>+</sup> channels open <b>or</b> Na<sup>+</sup> enters, muscle cell / sarcoplasm <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "3  <u>sarcolemma</u> depolarised <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "4  depolarisation / action potential, spreads, to / down, T-tubules <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "5  <b>voltage-gated</b> Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels open <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 6,
              "text": "6  Ca<sup>2+</sup>, diffuse / move, out of sarcoplasmic reticulum (and bind to troponin) <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Depolarisation spreads over the sarcolemma and into T-tubules, triggering calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium then binds troponin.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:15.1(10)",
                "ms:b#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Follow excitation from the neuromuscular junction into the fibre.",
            "Distinguish calcium release inside the muscle from transmitter release by the neurone."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Saying that acetylcholine binds directly to troponin",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:15.1(10)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "c",
        "label": "(c)",
        "source": {
